- op: discuss  # 73b1
  at: '2026-08-05T08:13:51Z'  # 73b1
  by: agent  # 73b1
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 73b1
  clock: 4  # 73b1
  nonce: 73b1  # 73b1
  message: "FIXED ON THE DDG PASS, PERMANENTLY LIMITED ON THE STRUCTURAL ONE. PR #214, dev 66798299bc.\nBEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE -- this item's OWN filed repro, three arms, same fixture, only the call\nstructure differing, re-run on merged dev through BOTH propagators (the filing does not say\nwhich one it used, and the answer now differs by pass, which is the point):\n  arm A, source+sanitizer+sink all in handler : structural sanitized=False/violated\n                                              : ddg        sanitized=True/confirmed_safe  <- the fix\n  arm B, sanitizer on an intermediate function: structural sanitized=True/confirmed_safe\n                                              : ddg        sanitized=True/confirmed_safe\n  arm C, no sanitizer at all (non-vacuity)    : both       sanitized=False/violated\nArm C is the floor: without it a fixture that cannot reach its sink would satisfy any assertion\nabout what the sanitizer does to it. Note arm B now reads 1 labelled finding rather than the\n0 findings this item recorded in August -- sanitized flows are emitted labelled since PR #195\ninstead of being pruned into silence, so the filed expected values moved under it.\nTHE BLOCKER THIS ITEM RECORDED WAS STALE AND IS NOT SECTION 3a. The filing says the real fix\nneeds statement ordering within the seed function and calls that section 3a. Section 3a is still\nconfirm-only (INV-sadah violated, WI-kabif todo_hard, declared constant inclusion_decided_by =\ncall_graph_reachability), but this item never needed 3a REFUTATION -- only the ordering, which\nlanded separately as stmt_defuse in PR #203 and was live at 13,930 functions / 144,070 statement\nrows. The data existed and did not reach the barrier.\nHOW IT IS FIXED. _ddg_taint_reaches gained a barrier_lines parameter and runs TWICE per\nsame-function candidate: unrestricted (is there a data route source to sink at all?) and with\nthe sanitizer call sites as barriers (does any route AVOID the barrier?). The sanitized label is\nearned only when the first returns True and the second returns False -- exhausted with every step\naccounted for. The barrier walk returning None never earns it: the value escaped tracked ground\non some route, and losing track of it is not the same as protecting it (L58 applied to\nsanitization rather than to removal). Marking a flow sanitized suppresses it from a claim\nviolation set, so it needs positive evidence exactly as section 3a removal arm does.\nMUTATION-TESTED, because six of the ten new tests passed BEFORE the fix and would otherwise be\nvacuous (L17). Four guards, each reddening exactly its own test and nothing else: accepting None\nas protection; dropping the confirmed-dependence precondition; making the barrier flag an escape\ninstead of accounting for it; claiming protection whenever any barrier exists in the function.\nA fifth mutation on cli.py (never compute the scope) reddens the end-to-end test, which is the\nL13 check that the unit-tested component is not inert in the pipeline.\nWHY THE STRUCTURAL PASS IS CLOSED RATHER THAN DEFERRED. propagate_taint_structural decides\nreachability on the call graph, where handler-calls-encrypt and handler-calls-write are two edges\nwith NO ORDER between them; the graph is byte-identical whichever order the source has, so no\nrefinement of call-graph reachability can distinguish encrypt-then-write from write-then-encrypt.\nThere is no remaining work on this item for that pass -- it is a scope limit, and every language\nwithout a def/use extractor is served by it, which is most of the catalogue. Rather than leave\nthat as folklore it is PUBLISHED per run as dataflow_coverage.sanitizer_scope.same_function_\nhonoured_by, currently [\"ddg\"], a declared constant whose test fails the moment the structural\npass gains the ordering it says it lacks (R16). Widening the set is the job of the per-language\ndef/use extractor items (WI-gotun for java, WI-nonad for javascript), not of this one.\nCOHORT EFFECT: ZERO, BY CONSTRUCTION, established WITHOUT running an A/B (L59). The fix fires\nonly where a sanitizer is REGISTERED against a function, so that gate was measured first with\nproduction own registrar: 0 sanitizer call sites across 95,950 taint call edges in all nine repos,\nwith a synthetic Fernet.encrypt edge confirming the instrument can fire (L9). Decomposed one level\nfurther because a zero has two readings with identical evidence: exactly ONE pre-gate name hit\nexists cohort-wide (pretix, a bare encrypt), refused correctly -- call_construct method on an\nunresolved edge is what the INV-finoh gate exists to reject, since binding a bare x.encrypt() to\nFernet.encrypt would falsely sanitize a real flow. The cohort zero is ABSENCE, not refusal.\nPrevention, not an improvement; value scales with the sanitizer catalogue, not with today output.\nKNOWN RESIDUAL, STATED RATHER THAN LEFT AS FOLKLORE. The barrier drops every heir at its line,\nwhich is wrong for a statement that BOTH sanitizes and rebinds the raw value (safe, leak =\nencrypt(plain), plain). The conservative alternative -- treat more than one heir as ambiguous --\nwas rejected on measurement, not taste: Go ct, err := aead.Seal(...) binds two names from EVERY\nsanitizer call in that language, so it would forfeit the fix wherever a multiple-return language\nis involved, trading a rare false negative for a systematic false positive, which is the defect\nbeing fixed here. The two cases are indistinguishable from the DDG alone (one statement row,\ndefines two names, uses one, in both). A sanitizer and sink on the SAME line, write(encrypt(x)),\nlikewise still reports unsanitized. Both are recorded in the code, CHANGELOG and commit message."  # 73b1
